**Parsing HTML with regex**

I'm not able to use BeautifulSoup, so I’ll have to parse the HTML using regex instead. I think I'll work with Python’s built-in HTML parser, or I might try the `html.parser`, but regex seems quickest for the `data-kit-h` spans. My plan is to capture elements with `data-kit-h="h\d+"` and extract the text. Although `lxml` may not be installed, I’ll stick with regex patterns for now. I’ll implement a simple regex approach to extract the necessary data.
exec
"C:\\Windows\\System32\\WindowsPowerShell\\v1.0\\powershell.exe" -Command "cd C:\\UI-specs\\URL-to-Code; @'
import re, pathlib
text = pathlib.Path('packs/fxfer-v3/screens/file-transfer-tracking/snapshot/rendered.html').read_text(encoding='utf-8')
pat = re.compile(r'<(?P<tag>\\w+)(?P<attrs>["'^>]*?'"\\sdata-kit-h=\"(?P<h>h\\d+)\"["'^>]*?'"\\sid=\"(?P<id>["'^"]+)"[''^>]*)>(?P<body>[''^<]*)</'"', re.I)
items=[]
for m in pat.finditer(text):
    h=m.group('h'); id_=m.group('id'); body=m.group('body').strip()
    items.append((int(h[1:]),h,id_,body))
items.sort()
for _,h,id_,body in items:
    print(f\"{h}\\t{id_}\\t{body}\")
print('count', len(items))
